        "DOI": "10.21203/rs.3.rs-11200633/v1",
        "title": [
          "Dynamic Multi-Objective Optimization for Batch-wise Task Assignment and Resource Allocation in Mobile Edge Computing"
        ],
        "issued": {
          "date-parts": [
            [
              2026,
              10,
              14
            ]
          ]
        },
        "is-referenced-by-count": 0,
        "abstract": "<title>Abstract</title>\n                <p>In practical mobile edge computing systems, computation tasks arrive in successive batches while task attributes, wireless conditions, and available resources vary across decision slots. This paper formulates batch-wise task assignment and communication/computing resource allocation as a constrained dynamic bi-objective optimization problem and proposes a Location-Resource Coupled Dynamic NSGA-II (LRC-DNSGA-II). Its Location-Resource Coupled Variation operator captures the dependency between discrete assignment and continuous allocation by restricting assignment mutation to accessible execution sites and resetting associated resource-weight genes when locations change. A task-feature-matched dynamic response mechanism reuses relevant decision information across successive batches. Experiments under dynamic batch-wise MEC scenarios show that LRC-DNSGA-II significantly outperforms four comparison methods in hypervolume and inverted generational distance. Ablation studies indicate that coupled variation mainly improves final approximation quality, whereas dynamic response primarily accelerates adaptation after batch transitions.</p>",
